---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Bug1 = `let count = 5;

while (count > 0) {
  console.log(count);
}

console.log("Liftoff!");`;
const Bug2 = `for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
  console.log(i);
}`;
const Bug3 = `let total = 0;

for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  total = i;
}

console.log("Total:", total);`;
const Bug4 = `for (let row = 1; row <= 3; row++) {
  let line = "";
  for (let col = 1; col <= 4; col++) {
    line = line + "*";
    console.log(line);
  }
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Debug Challenge: Loops</h1>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>Four short programs about repetition. One of them will not stop on its own, and part of this challenge is recognizing that <i>before</i> you run it.</p>

          <p>
            <b>Three kinds of mistake show up in JavaScript.</b> A{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-red">syntax error</b> stops the program before it
            runs. A <b class="text-sn-orange">reference error</b> runs until it
            hits the bad name, so you get <i>partial output and then a
            crash</i> &mdash; that partial output is how you tell it from a
            syntax error. A <b class="text-sn-yellow">logic error</b> runs
            cleanly to the end and gives the wrong answer, which is the one
            that will cost you marks on an exam.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>All four of these are logic errors</b> &mdash; not one of them
            produces an error message, including the one that never reaches
            the end. If you find yourself waiting for an error message, that is
            exactly the habit this challenge exists to break.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bugs">
          <h2>The Four Programs</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 1</h3>

          <p>This should count down from 5 to 1. It prints forever.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 1"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p><b>Also:</b> How would you know this loop never ends without running it? Say what you look for.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 2</h3>

          <p>This should print the numbers 1 through 10. It prints 0 through 9.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 2"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 3</h3>

          <p>This should add up 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 and print 15. It prints 5.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 3"
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Bug 4</h3>

          <p>This should print a 3 by 4 grid of stars &mdash; three rows of four. It prints twelve lines instead of three.</p>

          <Code
            code={Bug4}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Bug 4"
            class="mb-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For each program, give:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Which kind of mistake it is &mdash; syntax, reference, or logic</li>
            <li>One sentence on what was wrong</li>
            <li>The corrected line of code</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Work them out by reading first. Running the code tells you{" "}
            <i>that</i> something is wrong; it rarely tells you <i>why</i>, and
            on the exam there is no computer.
          </p>

          <p>Worth <b>3 points</b>, graded on completion.</p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Debugging
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
